WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.370
Film Courage: We interviewed a gentleman who
said a lot of Hollywood work is like temp

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:08.109
work, you are kind of swinging from one temp
job to another and that it’s never where

00:00:08.109 --> 00:00:09.309
you are really landing in one spot?

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:14.581
On the flip side you can say there are a lot
of people who have corporate jobs where they

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:19.540
were just given a severance package and pushed
out the door.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.080
So nothing is really guaranteed?

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.950
Andy Rydzewski, Cinematographer: Absolutely,
yes.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:26.500
It’s true.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:36.870
I think the nature of most film work or at
least the film work that I do there just isn’t

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.710
stability and there won’t be.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:46.610
I remember years ago, this probably would
have been 20 years ago I saw an interview

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:55.020
with Kevin Bacon and he is married to Kyra
Sedgwick and it was then and Kevin Bacon who

00:00:55.020 --> 00:01:01.980
has been around since I was a kid and he said
that after every project when it goes quiet

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:06.170
he gets very nervous and he’s like “Oh
no, am I ever going to work again?”

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:12.520
I remember that sticking out to me like wait
- Kevin Bacon is worried about getting work?

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:20.250
And now that I’ve been in the industry for
a little while - Oh right, you have no idea

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:27.040
if you will be, even if you’re on top of
the world two years from now maybe no one

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.560
remembers your name or you shoot a project
that bombs or whatever it is, it’s such

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:43.119
a competitive world and I don’t think it’s
a good industry to base your life on if you

00:01:43.119 --> 00:01:47.281
want to build a healthy family and I hate
saying that because it’s an industry that

00:01:47.281 --> 00:01:49.490
I love so dearly.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:57.970
But having a relationship when you’re going
80 hours a week and you come home and you’re…I

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:02.540
don’t know…you have to have a special
partnership to make that work.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:13.500
I think it’s very stressful on all your
relationships (romantic, friend, all of that).

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.920
I think that mostly I like to put that out
there.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:22.599
It doesn’t - nothing is good or bad it just
is and that’s like a fact of our industry

00:02:22.599 --> 00:02:28.170
and I think it’s important for people to
realize that because I think there are a lot

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:36.230
of romantic ideas going in but the reality
of it - and especially now - I don’t know,

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:38.890
will movie theaters open back up period?

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:44.540
I’m sure they’re going to open up in some
capacity but our world is changing.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:46.770
And streaming is now huge.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:55.640
I mean there are more TV shows being made
than ever in the history Hollywood but there

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:01.380
are no more residuals (almost entirely gone)
and that’s how a lot of writers and directors

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:06.580
and actors maintain a career is that you were
getting residuals from old projects but they

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:11.880
don’t have residuals on any of the streaming
services.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:16.319
So the future of our industry is very uncertain.

00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:24.830
But the flip-side is if you just look at the
art of making and between YouTube and all

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:31.230
these shows on Netflix and Hulu and Amazon
and all of these, there is more work and creation

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:38.569
being done than ever before it’s just the
money is the trick but money is always the

00:03:38.569 --> 00:03:39.780
trick I suppose.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:47.190
Film Courage: When you look at your IMDB and
your body of work, I’m surprise to hear

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:51.940
you question that but I think this is healthy
questioning though, just being realistic.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:58.780
Andy: I think that not taking it for granted
and never assuming that the future is going

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:06.019
to be what the present is because it probably
won’t be and you know I’ve only shot one

00:04:06.019 --> 00:04:20.589
project that anyone has ever heard of and
I think I’ve shot 10 seasons of TV and one

00:04:20.589 --> 00:04:23.810
was a hit to some capacity at least for its
size [PEN15 on Hulu].

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:27.430
Nobody has ever heard of the features I’ve
shot.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:33.789
You never know what is going to go in what
direction and so for me it’s really important

00:04:33.789 --> 00:04:39.430
that I love the work (the craft) which I do
because you have no control over the other

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:46.470
elements that of it and so I try to when I
can pay attention to who am I working with.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:51.559
The people make a real difference just in
terms of quality of life day to day.

00:04:51.559 --> 00:04:58.340
But you never know what’s going to work
or what’s not going to work or what anybody

00:04:58.340 --> 00:04:59.340
will find.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:07.379
I shot (I don’t remember now) four or five
series for go90 which was a Verizon streaming

00:05:07.379 --> 00:05:14.660
service which doesn’t exist anymore so even
the full streaming service (the channel) just

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:17.280
ceased to exist let alone those shows.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.830
But I love the work and I’m proud of the
work we did on a lot of those shows.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:29.169
I hope they find themselves a home because
some of them are really good shows but again

00:05:29.169 --> 00:05:35.690
I have no control over that so learning to
let go helps because you just control what

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:42.009
you can which is on set (on the day) you do
as good a job as you can, be pleasant to work

00:05:42.009 --> 00:05:47.650
with, enjoy it because that’s still your
life day to day there.

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:54.180
It’s weird, it’s very strange, it’s
depressing when I think about the realities

00:05:54.180 --> 00:06:02.029
of the industry, I tend to get pretty discouraged
because I would like to put some roots down,

00:06:02.029 --> 00:06:09.869
buy a house on day or whatever that might
look like and I don’t know when or if or

00:06:09.869 --> 00:06:12.099
how that will go?

00:06:12.099 --> 00:06:14.229
But for now I still love the work.

00:06:14.229 --> 00:06:18.499
Like I get excited, I just read a script this
week or a feature film that may or may not

00:06:18.499 --> 00:06:23.439
happen in the New Year and it’s like Oh
Yeah!

00:06:23.439 --> 00:06:24.889
How would I shoot this shot?

00:06:24.889 --> 00:06:26.610
What’s the look of this?

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:32.789
I still get really excited about that so I
let that lead me but I’m in my 40’s…

00:06:32.789 --> 00:06:34.479
Film Courage: Oh, you are?

00:06:34.479 --> 00:06:35.479
I did not know that.

00:06:35.479 --> 00:06:36.479
I thought you were much younger.

00:06:36.479 --> 00:06:37.979
You’ve been sleeping well!

00:06:37.979 --> 00:06:40.389
Andy: Yes, I’ve been sleep well.

00:06:40.389 --> 00:06:41.589
It’s a pandemic.

00:06:41.589 --> 00:06:47.770
Film Courage: Okay, well I’ll give you that…I’m
sure there are other folks out there who are

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:53.259
in a similar boat they are looking for work,
they’re ready for work but it’s not there

00:06:53.259 --> 00:06:54.889
or whatever the case may be.

00:06:54.889 --> 00:06:57.650
How do you keep yourself in check?

00:06:57.650 --> 00:06:59.709
You’ve said you have a meditation practice.

00:06:59.709 --> 00:07:05.740
Andy: Yes, so I know myself well enough at
this point and I’m still learning.

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:10.970
I haven’t figured out all of my quirks but
I know that if I don’t go for walks I will

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:12.889
start to go crazy.

00:07:12.889 --> 00:07:15.610
Going to the movie theater has always been
a big thing for me.

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:24.449
I always feel very at peace in a movie theater
but that is not an option these days.

00:07:24.449 --> 00:07:30.900
I have not gotten myself out to nature enough
but I know that a few days of camping will

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:39.539
usually reset me but how I eat and making
sure there is some exercise in my life and

00:07:39.539 --> 00:07:44.979
I almost take it like medicine, it has nothing
to day with “Oh, today is a good day to

00:07:44.979 --> 00:07:50.209
exercise” or “I feel like exercising,”
no - no - no - I have to because I will lose

00:07:50.209 --> 00:07:58.459
my mind otherwise and so a part of it is really
just being able to have the discipline I guess

00:07:58.459 --> 00:08:02.869
which for me feels strange to say because
I don’t think of myself as a disciplined

00:08:02.869 --> 00:08:10.709
person but for anything regarding filmmaking
I’ve always been able to convince myself

00:08:10.709 --> 00:08:12.650
to do something for the greater good.

00:08:12.650 --> 00:08:18.669
So keeping myself healthy for my own life
maybe doesn’t work but to keep myself healthy

00:08:18.669 --> 00:08:25.200
so that I’m a better filmmaker, so that
hour 13 I’m still energetic that’s some

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:34.899
how always been a way that I’ve been able
to keep myself happy and healthy to some degree

00:08:34.899 --> 00:08:39.659
and I’ve always struggled with anxiety and
so I have these little things that I know

00:08:39.659 --> 00:08:45.600
I need to do or I could lose a handle and
who knows just go crazy.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.470
Film Courage: Here’s Johnny!

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:49.350
Andy: Here’s Johnny, exactly.

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:54.670
Film Courage: I make light of it but we’re
all there in some way because this is uncharted

00:08:54.670 --> 00:09:05.209
territory and we thought we’d seen the worst
with The Great Recession and this has been

00:09:05.209 --> 00:09:07.170
a very trying year in so many ways.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:17.690
Andy: For sure and I will say gratitude - that’s
something I’m really trying to practice

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:19.579
is just being thankful for.

00:09:19.579 --> 00:09:25.440
I mean the fact that I shot a show that found
an audience and that people like, I am so

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:31.019
grateful for that after years of just grinding
and it felt like I was banging my head against

00:09:31.019 --> 00:09:33.269
the wall and then Oh!

00:09:33.269 --> 00:09:34.269
People like this!

00:09:34.269 --> 00:09:39.329
That’s great and it feels good but it’s
also like that’s already gone and so just

00:09:39.329 --> 00:09:44.720
to be grateful that something like that - grateful
for my health.

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:48.190
Anything that you can be grateful for I think
it goes a long way.

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:57.699
I think it’s easy to spiral in a global
pandemic, in a country that is pretty divided

00:09:57.699 --> 00:10:04.010
and a lot of anger and frustration, I think
- you know it’s dangerous, you don’t want

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:10.649
to ignore anything but it’s finding the
things that bring you some sort of peace and

00:10:10.649 --> 00:10:17.560
even joy I think is so important to remember
why do you want to live this life?

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:18.560
Why?

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:19.560
Oh yeah, right.

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:21.069
Like hugging, I remember hugging.

00:10:21.069 --> 00:10:22.069
Hugging is pretty good.

00:10:22.069 --> 00:10:28.519
I’m assuming we’ll do that one day so
yes, trying to be grateful for as much as

00:10:28.519 --> 00:10:35.470
you and when I moved into my new house I bought
myself a really nice TV and I was like Oh

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:36.470
this is really expensive.

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:41.970
I don’t know if I should but I did knowing
that I had a show coming and I was like Okay

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:48.379
I can rationalize the cost but now in the
pandemic I love that I have this huge TV and

00:10:48.379 --> 00:10:53.509
I’m watching all manner of things and it’s
a nice little treat and I can’t go to a

00:10:53.509 --> 00:10:59.279
movie theater but I have a nice set-up at
home which I didn’t have forever so you

00:10:59.279 --> 00:11:01.639
now I am thankful for that.

00:11:01.639 --> 00:11:03.579
Gratitude I think is a good, good tool.

00:11:03.579 --> 00:11:05.100
Hard to exercise but yeah…

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:09.639
Film Courage: What’s your favorite thing
to watch on this new TV now?

00:11:09.639 --> 00:11:18.110
Andy: You know what, I am all over the place
but I love watching a movie from the 70’s

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:26.709
or 80’s where it has this like beautiful
film grain and I love watching the grain because

00:11:26.709 --> 00:11:33.149
the TV is really nice and the blacks go really
black so if you watch somebody like Gordon

00:11:33.149 --> 00:11:40.459
Willis a legendary cinematographer his work
- he really leaned into - people called him

00:11:40.459 --> 00:11:44.829
‘The Prince of Darkness,’ and you watch
these images that are really dark but they

00:11:44.829 --> 00:11:52.010
have this grain dancing through it - film
grain - I have such like a romantic, nostalgic

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:58.470
reaction to it so it makes me really happy
to watch films with a ton of grain - dirt.

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:05.220
It’s funny, I have this clean TV and my
favorite things are the dirty images, I love

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:06.220
those.

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:08.209
Film Courage: That’s awesome.

00:12:08.209 --> 00:12:12.019
I love sometimes watching 80’s or 90’s
talk shows.

00:12:12.019 --> 00:12:19.800
I loved Phil Donahue, Jenny Jones, there is
just something very romantic about it.

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:25.329
It’s so nostalgic, Phil Donahue and the
hair styles.

00:12:25.329 --> 00:12:27.890
Andy: Totally, nostalgia is a superpower.

00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:30.329
Question For The Viewers: Is your view of
the film industry realistic or romantic?

